  
  Printing spread fast, since the machinery was easy to copy and the market was waiting. By 1500 presses operated in
  some 250 European towns, and printers had made an estimated 20 million volumes. Venice became the centre of the
  trade, and there Aldus Manutius introduced italic type and the small octavo format around 1500, which made books
  cheaper to produce and easier to carry. A scribe needed months to copy a Bible. A print shop could turn out hundreds
  in the same time, and prices fell accordingly. 
 
  Cheap print changed what people could know and who could argue. Martin Luther's Ninety-five Theses of 1517 were
  reprinted and passed across Germany within weeks, and his writings sold an estimated 300,000 copies by 1520. In 1543
  Copernicus and Vesalius both published, one on the heavens and one on the body, and print let readers compare
  identical diagrams and spot errors. Authors writing in German, English and French helped fix spelling and grammar in
  those languages. Rulers took notice. The Catholic Church issued its [0m[1mIndex of Prohibited Books[0m in 1559, and states in
  England and France required licences for presses. These controls slowed the flow but never stopped it.

  For three centuries the press itself barely changed. Then came iron frames, steam and mechanisation. In 1814 The
  Times of London installed Friedrich Koenig's steam-driven cylinder press and raised output to about 1,100 sheets an
  hour, several times the pace of a hand
